{"id":10,"course_id":3,"title":"YAML, Manifests, and kubectl Dry Runs","summary":"Read Kubernetes YAML safely and validate intent before mutation.","sequence":2,"body":"## Manifest basics\nKubernetes YAML usually has apiVersion, kind, metadata, spec, and sometimes status when read from the cluster. You write desired state in spec; Kubernetes writes observed state in status.\n- Indentation matters.\n- Lists and maps are different shapes; read them carefully.\n- Use dry-run and diff before applying unfamiliar YAML.\n- Generated manifests from Helm or Kustomize should be reviewed before cluster changes.\n\n## Local-safe commands\n$ kubectl apply --dry-run=client -f manifest.yaml\n$ kubectl diff -f manifest.yaml\n$ kubectl explain deployment.spec.template.spec.containers\n$ kubectl create deployment demo --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml\nIf you do not have a cluster, client dry-run and `kubectl explain` still teach object shape.\n\n## Common failure mode\nCopying YAML from a blog can create resources in the wrong namespace or with unsafe permissions.","practice_notes":"Scenario: You receive a 400-line manifest from a vendor.\nChecklist:\n- Identify every kind and namespace.\n- Search for ClusterRole, hostPath, privileged, LoadBalancer, and Secret.\n- Run client dry-run and schema validation if tools are available.\n- Ask what each cluster-scoped permission is for.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl apply --dry-run=client -f vendor.yaml\n$ grep -n \"kind:\\|namespace:\\|ClusterRole\\|privileged\\|hostPath\" vendor.yaml","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-cloud-native-foundations","course_category":"Cloud Native","course_era":"Platform Academy","terms":[{"id":46,"term":"Manifest","context":"Desired state","definition":"A YAML or JSON document describing Kubernetes resources."},{"id":47,"term":"apiVersion","context":"Kubernetes API","definition":"The API group and version used by a resource."},{"id":48,"term":"kind","context":"Resource type","definition":"The Kubernetes resource type, such as Deployment or Service."},{"id":49,"term":"spec","context":"Desired state","definition":"The part of an object where users declare what they want."},{"id":50,"term":"status","context":"Observed state","definition":"The part of an object where controllers report what happened."}],"flashcards":[{"id":28,"lesson_id":10,"prompt":"What fields appear in most Kubernetes manifests?","answer":"apiVersion, kind, metadata, and spec.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":29,"lesson_id":10,"prompt":"Why use dry-run?","answer":"To validate or preview a change without persisting it.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":30,"lesson_id":10,"prompt":"What should you inspect in vendor YAML?","answer":"Kinds, namespaces, cluster-scoped permissions, privileged settings, volumes, services, and secrets.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
